WATCH · DREAM · REMEMBER — credits and provenance

The three upgrades here — an independent regulator, an idle-time daydream tier, and per-mind memory with inherited
history — are inspired by Gary W. Floyd's NexusGrid, as he described it in conversation:
  Gary W. Floyd, Lumiea Systems Research Division — ThunderStruck Service LLC.
They are designed from that description only. No parity with his system is claimed. A comparison with his real
implementation is to follow his catch-up call; what it finds will be recorded here and on the page, differences included.

Where a build draws on one of his papers, the paper is credited at the point of use:
  Gary W. Floyd, Lumiea Systems Research Division — ThunderStruck Service LLC — "GEP-Based Security: Behavioral Entropy
    Signatures for Cybersecurity and DevOps," 2025 (CC BY 4.0) — the regulator's field E(t) = σ(k(|S − S_baseline| − ε)),
    the β|∇S| rate term, and the finding that scripted automation fails by being overly regular.
  Gary W. Floyd, Lumiea Systems Research Division — ThunderStruck Service LLC — "Dream State Architecture: GEP-Guided
    Memory Consolidation and Entropy Regulation in Artificial Consciousness Systems," 2025 — dream output kept INERT until
    a review gate validates it (the daydream log and the nightly gate).
His papers are published as Open Defensive Prior Art under Creative Commons.

Powered by the Konomi architecture, created by Thomas Frumkin, used with his permission. The daydream log and the minds'
life memory are kept in LIGHT, the estate's codec from that body of work (vendored from fall-kit, which vendors it from
konomi-tongue).

Data — the regulator is measured on the public commit histories of ten estate builds (tools/nodes.mjs: time, the part
of the build touched, and the number of files; no message, author or content). The daydream tier and the minds work on
five public datasets, read through dex-frontier's loaders at a pinned commit (tools/data.mjs), each CC BY 4.0:
  - Online Shoppers Purchasing Intention Dataset, C. Sakar and Y. Kastro, UCI Machine Learning Repository (2018),
    DOI 10.24432/C5F88Q.
  - Bank Marketing, UCI Machine Learning Repository, DOI 10.24432/C5K306. S. Moro, P. Cortez and P. Rita, A Data-Driven
    Approach to Predict the Success of Bank Telemarketing, Decision Support Systems 62 (2014).
  - Adult, UCI Machine Learning Repository, DOI 10.24432/C5XW20. B. Becker and R. Kohavi (1996).
  - Default of Credit Card Clients, UCI Machine Learning Repository, DOI 10.24432/C55S3H. I.-C. Yeh and C.-H. Lien,
    Expert Systems with Applications 36(2) (2009).
  - In-Vehicle Coupon Recommendation, UCI Machine Learning Repository, DOI 10.24432/C5GS4P. T. Wang, C. Rudin,
    F. Doshi-Velez, Y. Liu, E. Klampfl and P. MacNeille, Journal of Machine Learning Research 18(70) (2017).

Minds here are models of thought-patterns: never the people they are trained on, and never claimed to be conscious.

